internal/store/store.go:769-774 (new in this PR):
```go
fetch := opts.Offset + opts.Limit
if opts.Limit == 0 {
    fetch = total // limit 0 = "all" (preserves prior behavior)
}
if fetch == 0 {
    // Qdrant's Scroll rejects Limit=0 outright ("must be 1 or larger"), and
    // there is nothing to fetch anyway (total==0 or an explicit zero-width
    // request) -- short-circuit rather than round-trip for an empty page.
    return []Memory{}, total, "", nil
}
```
The comment claims two distinct causes for fetch==0: 'total==0' or 'an explicit zero-width request'. Tracing the arithmetic, the second cause is not actually reachable: fetch starts as opts.Offset+opts.Limit; if opts.Limit != 0 that sum is always > 0 (uint64, no request path sets a negative/zero Limit distinct from the 'omit Limit' default), so fetch can only be overwritten to 0 via the opts.Limit==0 branch, which sets fetch = total. So fetch==0 in practice only ever means opts.Limit==0 (the documented 'all' sentinel) AND total==0 -- i.e. the scope is empty. There is no request shape that reaches this branch via a literal 'zero-width request' distinct from the empty-scope case. Suggest tightening the comment to describe the actual single trigger (Limit==0 'all' request against an empty/zero-total scope) rather than implying a second, non-existent code path -- a future reader trying to construct the 'explicit zero-width request' case will not find one.

internal/server/tools.go (getMemory ~L821-835, deleteMemory ~L839-855, setVisibility ~L859-875, updateMemory's FetchForUpdate branch ~L785-791, storeDiscovery's OwnedOrAbsent branch ~L577-585) and internal/server/connectapi.go GetMemory (~L191-199) all repeat the identical 3-line idiom, each with its own copy of the same explanatory comment:

if errors.Is(err, store.ErrNotFound) {
    return <zero-values>, fmt.Errorf("%w: %s", store.ErrNotFound, a.ID)  // 'never leaks another owner's real UUID'
}
return <zero-values>, err

This is not the 'resolve id-or-short-id' step itself (which legitimately differs per caller and is reasonably left explicit per repo style) — it's specifically the not-found re-wrap tail, which is byte-identical logic repeated 5-6 times along with a byte-identical rationale comment. A tiny helper collapses all of them to one line each and centralizes the one comment that needs to stay correct:

// wrapNotFoundWithOriginal re-wraps a not-found error with the caller's
// original id/short_id input, so a resolved short id never leaks another
// owner's real UUID into the error message. Other errors pass through unchanged.
func wrapNotFoundWithOriginal(err error, original string) error {
    if errors.Is(err, store.ErrNotFound) {
        return fmt.Errorf("%w: %s", store.ErrNotFound, original)
    }
    return err
}

Example call-site simplifications (behavior-preserving):
- deleteMemory: 'return wrapNotFoundWithOriginal(d.st.Delete(ctx, pid, subj), a.ID)'
- getMemory: 'm, err := d.st.GetReadable(ctx, pid, subj); return m, wrapNotFoundWithOriginal(err, a.ID)'
- updateMemory/storeDiscovery: 'if err != nil { return ..., wrapNotFoundWithOriginal(err, a.ID) }'
- connectapi.go GetMemory: wrap err with wrapNotFoundWithOriginal(err, req.Msg.Id) before the errors.Is/connect.NewError branching.

This is offered as a judgment call given the repo's stated preference for explicit code over DRY ceremony — flagging because the duplication here is literal (identical code + identical comment, not just structurally similar), and a future fix to the leak-prevention logic currently requires editing 5-6 sites in lockstep. If the reviewer prefers the current explicit repetition, this can be closed as won't-fix.

