The PR splits body construction into two branches:

    var body []byte
    if len(params) == 0 {
        body, _ = json.Marshal(embedReq{Model: c.model, Input: text})
    } else {
        m := make(map[string]any, len(params)+2)
        for k, v := range params {
            m[k] = v
        }
        m["model"] = c.model
        m["input"] = text
        body, _ = json.Marshal(m)
    }

The comment justifying the split says: 'Empty params → marshal the struct (exact prior wire bytes; default path) ... Go sorts map keys on marshal; that is JSON-semantically identical, so callers compare decoded objects, not raw bytes.'

That comment's own conclusion undercuts the branch: if nothing (in this codebase or realistically any conformant JSON consumer) actually compares raw bytes — and the new tests (TestEmbedParamsMergedIntoBody, TestEmbedDocumentInstruction, captureBody/captureInput helpers) all decode the body via json.Decode/json.NewDecoder, never bytes.Equal against a literal — then the struct-vs-map branch buys nothing observable. It only exists to keep 'model' before 'input' in the wire bytes for the params-empty case, which no caller (test, gateway, or otherwise) in this repo depends on.

This adds a permanent branch + duplicated field list (model/input appear in both the embedReq struct tags and the map literal) for a distinction that is only skin-deep. A single code path — always build the map (a no-op range over nil/empty params leaves just model+input) — removes the branch and the up-front embedReq/map duplication:

    m := make(map[string]any, len(params)+2)
    for k, v := range params {
        m[k] = v
    }
    m["model"] = c.model
    m["input"] = text
    body, _ := json.Marshal(m)

Trade-off to flag explicitly if proposing this: the default (no-params) case's wire key order would change from {"model":...,"input":...} (struct field order) to {"input":...,"model":...} (map keys, alphabetically sorted by encoding/json) — semantically identical JSON, but a literal byte diff. If the team has an actual reason to pin the default path's byte-for-byte wire format (e.g. an external byte-comparing consumer not visible in this repo), keep the split but say so in the comment instead of the current self-defeating justification; otherwise consolidate to one path.

Suggested fix: either (a) collapse to the single map-based path above, or (b) if byte stability for the default case is a real requirement, rewrite the comment to state that requirement plainly rather than asserting 'callers compare decoded objects, not raw bytes' immediately before using that same fact to justify keeping two code paths.

