File: internal/store/store.go, lines 587-599 (ListOptions) and lines 681-701 (List routing).

The two paging modes — offset and cursor — are encoded across three fields in ListOptions:

    Cursor     string  // "" = offset mode; non-empty = cursor mode
    CursorMode bool    // true = cursor paging (MCP default); false = offset paging
    Offset     uint64

The struct comment on Cursor says the modes are mutually exclusive with Offset>0, but only the combination (Cursor!="" && Offset>0) is runtime-guarded:

    if opts.Cursor != "" && opts.Offset > 0 {
        return nil, 0, "", fmt.Errorf("list: cursor and offset are mutually exclusive")
    }

The routing condition that follows is:

    if opts.Cursor != "" || (opts.Offset == 0 && opts.Limit > 0 && opts.CursorMode) {
        items, nextCursor, err = s.listByCursor(ctx, f, opts)
    }

A caller who sets CursorMode=true, Cursor="", Offset=5 hits neither guard: CursorMode=true is silently ignored and offset paging runs. No error is returned; the CursorMode intent is lost. The invariant "CursorMode=true implies Offset==0" is comment-only.

This is not reachable from current callers (MCP always sends Offset=0; Connect sets CursorMode only when PageToken != ""), but the struct admits the invalid state without any compile-time or early-runtime rejection.

Suggested fix: Replace the three-field encoding with a mode enum, e.g.:

    type PagingMode uint8
    const (
        OffsetPagingMode PagingMode = iota
        CursorPagingMode
    )
    // Then in ListOptions:
    //   Paging  PagingMode
    //   Offset  uint64  // meaningful only when Paging == OffsetPagingMode
    //   Cursor  string  // meaningful only when Paging == CursorPagingMode

This makes the modes structurally exclusive and removes the need for the runtime guard. At minimum, add a guard for the (CursorMode=true && Cursor=="" && Offset>0) case and return an error rather than silently demoting to offset mode.

