RemapOwner calls src.Validate(to) at the top. buildRemapSource constructs the struct first, calls .Validate(to), and wraps or passes through the error (CLI-specific phrasing can still override: check for the specific error or re-phrase at the CLI layer). This eliminates the duplicate counting logic while keeping the fail-fast call site in the CLI.

Location: internal/store/store.go (OwnerRemapSource, ~line 764) and cmd/engram/migrate.go (buildRemapSource, ~line 59).

OwnerRemapSource is a 3-way tagged union expressed as an exported struct with three public fields (Missing bool, Anon bool, From string). The documented invariant — 'exactly one of the three must be set' — is enforced only at runtime in two separate functions: buildRemapSource (cmd layer) and RemapOwner (store layer). The struct's zero value (OwnerRemapSource{}) violates the invariant and is silently constructible. Combinations like OwnerRemapSource{Missing: true, Anon: true} or all three set are also silently constructible.

The contrast is pointed: the same package already demonstrates the correct Go pattern via the Subject type — a sealed interface with unexported concrete variants (anonymous{}/authenticated{}) and named constructors (Anonymous()/Authenticated()) that make invalid states unconstructible.

Suggested fix: close the type with unexported fields and per-variant constructors:

  type OwnerRemapSource struct { kind uint8; from string }
  func RemapMissing() OwnerRemapSource   { return OwnerRemapSource{kind: 1} }
  func RemapAnon() OwnerRemapSource      { return OwnerRemapSource{kind: 2} }
  func RemapFrom(v string) OwnerRemapSource { return OwnerRemapSource{kind: 3, from: v} }

RemapOwner can then switch on src.kind directly, collapsing the duplicate counter-based validation in both layers into a single exhaustive match. The zero value becomes self-documenting (kind==0 means 'not set') and returns a clear error without the counting dance.

Note: the current duplicate validation (cmd + store) does prevent actual data corruption, so this is a design quality issue rather than a live bug path.

