File: ui/src/lib/markdown.ts, line 46.

The call:
  const html = marked.parse(src, { async: false }) as string;

is redundant. marked.d.ts (lib/marked.d.ts:699-701) declares a dedicated overload:
  marked(src: string, options: MarkedOptions & { async: false }): string;

and marked.parse is typed as typeof marked (line 730), so all overloads apply. TypeScript already resolves the call to string without the cast.

The cast is harmless today, but it creates a forward-risk: if someone later changes the option to { async: true } or removes it (falling through to the union overload returning string | Promise<string>), the 'as string' suppresses the compile-time error rather than surfacing it. The runtime consequence would be DOMPurify.sanitize() receiving a Promise object, producing either '[object Promise]' in the rendered HTML or unpredictable sanitizer behavior — a silent regression in a security-sensitive pipeline.

Suggested fix: remove the cast and rely on the overload.
  const html = marked.parse(src, { async: false });

The return type will be string as before, and any future async drift will now be a compile error.

