In internal/summarize/summarize.go:Summarize (around line 112), when the gateway returns a non-200 status the function returns immediately with an error, relying on the deferred resp.Body.Close() to clean up:

    if resp.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
        return "", fmt.Errorf("chat completions: status %d", resp.StatusCode)
    }

However, closing an HTTP response body without reading it to EOF prevents the underlying TCP connection from being reused (per net/http docs). For the summarize-missing sweep over large record sets, if the gateway returns repeated errors (rate limit, quota, etc.), each error will leak a connection from the pool, potentially exhausting the pool.

Additionally, the error message omits the response body content, making gateway-side error messages invisible to the operator.

Suggested fix:
    if resp.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
        body, _ := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(resp.Body, 512))
        return "", fmt.Errorf("chat completions: status %d: %s", resp.StatusCode, strings.TrimSpace(string(body)))
    }

This drains the body (enabling connection reuse) and surfaces the gateway error message in logs.

File: internal/store/store.go, lines 637-639 (PR diff)

The new state.valid() guard returns an error before the OTel span is started (line 640) and before telemetry.RecordStoreOp is wired into the defer (line 646-654). The error is correctly returned to the caller (not swallowed), but the call is entirely invisible to observability: no store.ListScheduled span is emitted and no StoreOp metric is recorded.

