File: internal/store/store.go, lines 1218-1260.

The scroll loop uses an infinite for{} with a trailing break on nil next:

  var offset *qdrant.PointId
  for {
      pts, next, err = s.client.ScrollAndOffset(...)
      if err != nil { return ... }
      for _, p := range pts { ... }
      if next == nil {
          break
      }
      offset = next
  }

The two-statement tail (if next == nil { break }; offset = next) and the offset var declared outside the loop are the only things tying the loop together. The break-at-tail pattern requires the reader to scan to the bottom of the loop to understand the termination condition.

A cleaner equivalent uses the first return of ScrollAndOffset to drive a do-while-style structure by keeping the same semantics but making offset management collocated:

  for offset := (*qdrant.PointId)(nil); ; offset = next {
      pts, next, err = s.client.ScrollAndOffset(ctx, &qdrant.ScrollPoints{..., Offset: offset, ...})
      if err != nil { return res, fmt.Errorf(...) }
      for _, p := range pts { ... }
      if next == nil {
          break
      }
  }

Or alternatively, declare next outside and use it as the loop variable:

  var next *qdrant.PointId
  for {
      var pts []*qdrant.RetrievedPoint
      pts, next, err = ...
      ...
      if next == nil { break }
      offset = next
  }

The current form is idiomatic Go for Qdrant-style cursor pagination and not wrong, but collocating offset = next and the nil-check would make the cursor-update pattern explicit. Filed as suggestion; the existing loop is readable to anyone familiar with the pattern.

