File: internal/store/subject.go line 39.

The authenticated variant carries the invariant sub != "", but Authenticated(sub string) has no guard:

    func Authenticated(sub string) Subject { return authenticated{sub: sub} }

Calling Authenticated("") constructs a value that is typed as authenticated (not anonymous) but whose sub field is "". This breaks both the write gate and the read filter:

Write gate (getWritable / OwnedOrAbsent / FetchForUpdate): the authenticated arm fires and evaluates m.Owner != sj.sub == m.Owner != "". For anonymous-bucket records (owner==""), this is false — the gate passes. Semantically identical to Anonymous(), but the type tag says authenticated. A caller that constructs Authenticated("") gets anonymous-bucket write access.

Read filter (ownerOrSharedCondition): the authenticated arm builds a Should filter of owner==sub (i.e. owner=="") OR visibility=="shared". This matches anonymous-bucket records AND all shared records — a wider grant than Anonymous() (which matches owner=="" only). A Authenticated("") read query would leak all shared records to a caller that should be treated as anonymous.

The production subjectFromContext guards this correctly (checks sub != "" before calling the constructor), so no normal OIDC flow produces Authenticated(""). However, the invariant is unenforced at the package boundary: any external caller, test author, or future code path can invoke it silently.

Suggested fix:
    func Authenticated(sub string) Subject {
        if sub == "" {
            panic("store.Authenticated: sub must not be empty")
        }
        return authenticated{sub: sub}
    }

Or at minimum add a precondition to the doc comment. A panic is preferable because it makes the invariant visible at construction time rather than only at the enforcement site.

Note: this issue is also tracked under engram-7cu.1 (aspect:code); this bead captures the type-design dimension — the constructor failing to enforce the authenticated variant's own invariant.

