The substantive content of the comments is purposeful and should be kept; only the unicode decorators need removal.

internal/store/store.go, internal/server/tools.go — The authz model uses sub=="" as the anonymous-bucket sentinel across OwnedForUpdate, getWritable, ownerOrSharedCondition, GetReadable, and OwnedOrAbsent. ownerFromContext correctly guards against authenticated-but-empty-sub, and the PR's new anonymous-bucket logic is consistently applied.

However, Go's zero value for string is "", so any callsite that constructs a sub through a code path that bypasses ownerFromContext (e.g. a future internal batch job, a migration helper, or a test that forgets to set owner) silently inherits anonymous-bucket semantics rather than failing visibly.

A stronger design would be a discriminated type:
  type Subject struct{ sub string; anon bool }
  func AnonymousSubject() Subject { return Subject{anon: true} }
  func AuthenticatedSubject(sub string) Subject { return Subject{sub: sub} }

This makes the anonymous/authenticated distinction unconstructable-by-accident and all switch points compile-checked. Flagging as a suggestion because it is a larger refactor of a stable, well-tested surface — not a blocking issue in this PR.

cmd/engram/httplog.go:29,43-45

accessLog accepts observe func(int) as a parameter and nil-checks it on every request in the production hot path. The comment explicitly calls it a test seam; in production serve.go:82 passes nil.

The nil-check in the hot path is a code smell: production code explicitly handles a nil case that only exists for tests.

Simpler alternative: default to a no-op func if nil is passed, moving the nil-check to the closure setup rather than the per-request path:

  if observe == nil {
      observe = func(int) {}
  }
  return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler { ... }

This eliminates the per-request branch entirely. Alternatively, the test could pass a real (non-nil) no-op func. Either way the hot-path nil-check disappears.

File: internal/telemetry/logger.go, function fanoutHandler.Handle (lines 82-92).

The fanout handler iterates over all wrapped handlers and captures only the first error (firstErr), silently discarding errors from all subsequent handlers. If the stdout slog.JSONHandler fails (disk full, closed pipe) and the otelslog bridge also returns an error, only the first is returned; the second is dropped with no log, no counter, no trace.

While slog.Handler.Handle errors are advisory in practice (the stdlib slog infrastructure does not propagate them to callers), returning only the first error creates an asymmetry: callers who do inspect the error (e.g., tests, future wrappers) see an incomplete picture. More concretely, if only the second handler errors, the first-error guard means it is silently swallowed with zero signal.

Suggested fix: accumulate errors with errors.Join so all handler failures are reported together:

  func (f *fanoutHandler) Handle(ctx context.Context, r slog.Record) error {
      var errs []error
      for _, h := range f.handlers {
          if h.Enabled(ctx, r.Level) {
              if err := h.Handle(ctx, r.Clone()); err != nil {
                  errs = append(errs, err)
              }
          }
      }
      return errors.Join(errs...)
  }

This is a low-severity suggestion since slog handler errors are rarely acted on in production, but the current code is inconsistent with the multi-handler design intent.

